SOFA v25.12 core Ogden material (TetrahedronHyperelasticityFEMForceField, materialName
"Ogden"): wrong stress and stiffness where two principal stretches coincide.

Since 17/11/2025 Ogden::precomputeVariables takes C's eigenvectors from Eigen's general
(non-symmetric) EigenSolver, then builds C^(alpha1/2 - 1) = V D V^T, i.e. uses V^T as
V's inverse. That holds only for orthonormal V. Where two eigenvalues of C coincide, or
differ by rounding only, the general solver's eigenvectors in that plane are not
orthogonal, and V D V^T (and the elasticity tensor, built from the same V) is wrong.
Measured with Eigen 3 on C = (F R)^T (F R), F plus noise of size e, R a random rotation,
alpha1 = 6, largest error of V D V^T over 20,000 samples, relative to |C^2|:

    F                    e = 1e-16   1e-12     1e-8
    diag(1, 1, 1)        186%        3.6%      1.9e-6     (rest)
    diag(1, 0.9, 1)      97%         0.71%     5.8e-7     (uniaxial / confined compression)
    diag(1.05,1.05,0.9)  96%         1.6%      1.1e-6     (axisymmetric, e.g. under a round probe)
    diag(1.1, 0.9, 1)    8e-15       7e-15     7e-15      (distinct stretches: correct)

SelfAdjointEigenSolver with Eigen::ComputeEigenvectors gives orthonormal eigenvectors;
with it (or with V D V^-1) every case above is exact to 1e-14. Found in this project by
comparing SOFA's CPU Ogden with an independent GPU implementation in confined
compression (README section 16). Apply from the SOFA source root:
    patch -p1 < SOFA-Ogden-orthonormal-eigenvectors.patch

--- a/Sofa/Component/SolidMechanics/FEM/HyperElastic/src/sofa/component/solidmechanics/fem/hyperelastic/material/Ogden.h
+++ b/Sofa/Component/SolidMechanics/FEM/HyperElastic/src/sofa/component/solidmechanics/fem/hyperelastic/material/Ogden.h
@@ -79,17 +79,21 @@
             for (sofa::Index j = 0; j < 3; ++j) 
                 CEigen(i, j) = C[MatrixSym::voigtID(i, j)];
 
-        // 17/11/2025: Disable /*Eigen::SelfAdjointEigenSolver<EigenMatrix>*/
-        // due to incorrect eigenvector computation for 3x3 matrices.
-        Eigen::EigenSolver<Eigen::Matrix<Real, 3, 3> > EigenProblemSolver(CEigen, true);
+        // C is symmetric, and C^(alpha1/2 - 1) is built below as V D V^T: V must be
+        // orthonormal. Eigen's general EigenSolver does not make it so where two
+        // eigenvalues (nearly) coincide - at rest, in uniaxial or axisymmetric states -
+        // and V D V^T is then wrong by up to 100%. SelfAdjointEigenSolver returns
+        // orthonormal eigenvectors, but only with Eigen::ComputeEigenvectors: its second
+        // argument is an option flag, and `true` (= 1) asks for none.
+        Eigen::SelfAdjointEigenSolver<EigenMatrix> EigenProblemSolver(CEigen, Eigen::ComputeEigenvectors);
         if (EigenProblemSolver.info() != Eigen::Success)
         {
             dmsg_warning("Ogden") << "EigenSolver iterations failed to converge";
             return;
         }
 
-        sinfo->Evalue = EigenProblemSolver.eigenvalues().real().eval();
-        sinfo->Evect = EigenProblemSolver.eigenvectors().real().eval();
+        sinfo->Evalue = EigenProblemSolver.eigenvalues();
+        sinfo->Evect = EigenProblemSolver.eigenvectors();
 
         const Real aBy2{alpha1/static_cast<Real>(2)};
         m_trCaBy2 = static_cast<Real>(0);
